
Software architecture is about deciding how a large application should be structured.

At a beginner level you think:

    "How do I write this function?"

At an advanced level you start thinking:

    "How should the entire system be organised?"

Architecture becomes increasingly important as applications grow.

    Small program
        ↓
    Multiple modules
        ↓
    Large application
        ↓
    Multiple services
        ↓
    Distributed system

The goal is not to make everything complicated.

The goal is to make complexity manageable.

---

## SOLID

SOLID is a collection of five principles for designing maintainable object-oriented software.

    S → Single Responsibility Principle
    O → Open/Closed Principle
    L → Liskov Substitution Principle
    I → Interface Segregation Principle
    D → Dependency Inversion Principle

---

## Single Responsibility Principle

A class or module should have one clear responsibility.

Bad:

    class User:
        create_account()
        send_email()
        generate_invoice()
        save_to_database()

This class is responsible for too many unrelated things.

Better:

    User
        ↓
    UserService
        ↓
    EmailService
        ↓
    InvoiceService
        ↓
    UserRepository

Each component has a clearer responsibility.

The idea is:

    One component
        ↓
    One main reason to change

---

## Open/Closed Principle

Software should be:

    Open for extension
    Closed for modification

You should ideally be able to add new behaviour without constantly changing existing, tested code.

For example, instead of:

    if payment_type == "card":
        ...

    elif payment_type == "paypal":
        ...

    elif payment_type == "bitcoin":
        ...

you might define a common payment interface and create separate implementations.

    Payment
      ├── CardPayment
      ├── PayPalPayment
      └── BitcoinPayment

New payment methods can then be added without rewriting the entire payment system.

---

## Liskov Substitution Principle

Objects of a subclass should be usable wherever the parent type is expected without breaking the program.

Conceptually:

    Parent
      ↑
    Child

If code expects the parent, replacing it with the child should still work correctly.

Bad inheritance often occurs when a subclass technically fits the class hierarchy but does not actually behave like the parent.

The principle encourages meaningful abstractions rather than inheritance just for the sake of reusing code.

---

## Interface Segregation Principle

Classes should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not need.

Instead of creating one enormous interface:

    EverythingInterface
        print()
        scan()
        fax()
        email()
        copy()
        ...

split it into smaller responsibilities:

    Printable
    Scannable
    Faxable
    Emailable

Components only implement what they actually need.

---

## Dependency Inversion Principle

High-level code should not depend directly on low-level implementation details.

Instead:

    High-level logic
          ↓
      abstraction
          ↓
    Low-level implementation

For example:

    OrderService
          ↓
    PaymentProcessor
          ↓
    StripePayment

The order system depends on the concept of payment processing rather than being permanently tied to one specific payment provider.

